WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.020
Settle-in, this is unintended consequences, episode 68 for July 24, 2026, closing the week with one

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:15.320
final case study before the weekend. In the spring of 2018, a man named Robert Williams was arrested

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:21.660
in his Detroit driveway after a routine errand. A facial recognition match had linked him to a

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:27.860
department store theft he had not committed, and officers took him into custody on that basis alone.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:33.580
The system responsible had first been trained and deployed for border and airport checks,

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:39.180
where officials could argue that a single high stakes identification justified tolerating

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:44.820
a measurable error rate because the alternative was slower manual inspection of every traveler.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:51.380
Now the same capability running on ordinary police cameras with no additional accuracy requirements

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:57.060
produced a false match that turned an everyday errand into a criminal booking and all the

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:02.820
downstream consequences that follow from an arrest record. After the attacks of September 11,

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:09.960
2001, US and European officials looked for ways to verify identities at borders without lengthening

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.480
already lengthy lines that were already frustrating passengers and costing airlines money in delays.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:21.400
The Transportation Security Administration and Customs and Border Protection began testing

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:26.520
automated facial comparison to confirm that the person boarding a plane matched the passport

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:32.380
photo already on file, reasoning that this step could replace some of the visual checks performed

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:38.640
by agents. At roughly the same time, phone makers faced a different pressure from consumers who

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.040
wanted faster unlock methods than typing pins on small screens while still keeping the device

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:52.460
secure against casual access. Apple's 2017 introduction of Face ID on the iPhone X was presented as a

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:58.320
convenience feature that also raised the bar for device security by requiring a three-dimensional map

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:05.080
of the face rather than a simple image. In both settings, the stated goal was the same. Reduce

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:11.040
friction for legitimate travelers and users while making impersonation harder for anyone who did not

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:16.920
match the stored reference. The engineers and policy makers involved worked with the data they had

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:23.160
at the time, which meant controlled lighting at airport gates, cooperative subjects holding still for

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:29.660
a few seconds, and databases limited to known travelers or device owners. Under those constraints,

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:35.460
the accuracy claims appeared reasonable because the conditions matched the training data. But that

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:40.780
match between lab conditions and real use would not necessarily travel when the same algorithms

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:49.360
moved elsewhere. Between 2013 and 2017, customs and border protection ran pilot programs at airports

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:55.660
in Atlanta, New York, and Miami that compared departing passengers' faces against passport images,

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:03.220
processing each traveler through cameras mounted at boarding gates. By 2018, the agency had expanded

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:08.820
the effort to more than a dozen U.S. airports and was sharing the underlying traveler data with

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:14.840
airlines for boarding, which lowered the per passenger cost once the cameras and matching software

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:22.100
were already in place. On the commercial side, Apple's Face ID required users to enroll voluntarily

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:27.880
and kept the facial template on the device itself so that no central database was created.

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:33.820
Police departments, however, began feeding the same class of algorithms with mug shot databases

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:40.180
and street camera feeds, which introduced different lighting, angles, and demographic mixes than

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:47.800
the airport gates had provided. A 2018 study by Joy Buowel-Winney and Timnett Gaybrue at MIT

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:53.880
documented large accuracy gaps across skin tones and genders in several commercial systems

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:59.780
than in use, showing that error rates rose when the faces being matched differed from the narrower

00:03:59.780 --> 00:04:05.780
set of images used to train the models. Vendors responded that their latest models performed

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:11.860
better under controlled conditions with even lighting and cooperative poses. Critics noted that

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:17.420
real-world lighting, camera angles, and demographic distributions rarely matched those conditions,

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:22.860
especially once the same systems left the airport environment. Once the cameras,

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:29.260
databases, and matching software existed, the marginal cost of pointing them at new populations

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:34.820
fell sharply, because the hardware was already purchased, the data pipelines were already built,

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:41.360
and the algorithms were already running. Retailers tested the technology to identify known shoplifters

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:47.720
by feeding store security footage into the same matching process. Sports venues used it to eject

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:53.940
band fans, again relying on existing camera networks rather than installing entirely new infrastructure.

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:59.800
School districts and several states installed cameras marketed for visitor management that could

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:05.880
also flag students, extending the reach without requiring a fresh public debate about accuracy

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:13.100
thresholds. In London's Kings Cross development, a network of cameras ran facial recognition for years

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:19.540
before public disclosure. Illustrating how the same capability could operate quietly once the initial

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:26.180
justification of security or efficiency was accepted. In China, the same infrastructure built for airport

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:32.140
security and phone payments was repurposed for tracking weaker populations and monitoring public

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:38.460
demonstrations. Showing how quickly the technology could shift from cooperative checkpoint use to broad

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:44.820
surveillance once the cameras and databases were already deployed. Each new use began with a plausible

00:05:44.820 --> 00:05:51.100
safety or efficiency argument, yet the systems carried forward the original error rates and demographic

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:56.920
biases because nothing in the technical stack forced a re-evaluation when the context changed.

00:05:57.480 --> 00:06:03.340
A false match at an airport might delay a traveler for additional screwing. The same error in a

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:09.460
policing context could produce an arrest or in some jurisdictions prolonged detention because the

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:15.180
downstream consequences of a match were now handled by different institutions that had not participated

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:21.860
in the original accuracy testing. Because the technology operated passively, individuals rarely

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:26.720
knew when their faces were being compared or how long the resulting records would be stored,

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:33.020
which removed the feedback loop that might have prompted earlier corrections. Secondary markets

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:40.100
emerged as a result. Clearview AI scraped billions of web photos to sell facial search services to law

00:06:40.100 --> 00:06:46.540
enforcement. Extending the reach of systems originally justified for border control by treating publicly

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:53.720
posted images as another ready made database. The original high stakes low volume logic no longer

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:59.900
constrained deployment once the capability was cheap and the data already collected because the decision

00:06:59.900 --> 00:07:05.840
to add a new camera feed or a new list of faces carried almost no additional technical cost.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:11.300
Public backlash produced a patchwork of responses rather than a uniform set of rules.

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:18.260
San Francisco and several other cities banned municipal use of facial recognition, while the European

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:24.380
Union considered restrictions in its AI act that would differentiate between high risk and lower risk

00:07:24.380 --> 00:07:30.200
applications. Some airlines quietly scaled back boarding gate matching after passenger complaints

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:36.460
about the process and about data handling. Accuracy improved in vendor benchmarks yet independent

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:43.240
audits continued to show gaps, especially for darker skin tones and an outdoor or crowded settings where

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:48.780
lighting and angles varied more than at a controlled gate. Police departments that continued the

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:55.360
practice added human review layers, but those reviews themselves introduced new delays and discretionary

00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:01.700
decisions about which matches to accept or discard. The infrastructure, cameras already mounted,

00:08:02.020 --> 00:08:07.720
databases already populated, remained in place, making future expansion technically straightforward

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:14.600
even where policy temporarily narrowed it. Today the technology operates under differing rules by

00:08:14.600 --> 00:08:21.260
country and by use case, with no single standard governing retention, consent, or cross-purpose data

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:28.100
sharing. Capabilities built for one narrow high-stakes setting tend to migrate to lower-stakes environments

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:34.280
where the original accuracy and oversight assumptions no longer hold, because the hardware and data

00:08:34.280 --> 00:08:41.140
are already available and the marginal cost of reuse is low. Once cameras and matching infrastructure

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:47.220
exist, the decision to apply them elsewhere is driven by convenience and cost rather than renewed

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:52.080
public debate about whether the new setting justifies the same error tolerances.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:59.460
Listeners facing any new sensing or data system can ask what happens to the same hardware and records

00:08:59.460 --> 00:09:05.520
when the initial justification no longer limits their use. The question is not whether the first

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:10.860
deployment was reasonable, but whether the second and third deployments will be governed by equally

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:17.120
careful constraints. Consider any new workplace monitoring tool rolled out for safety compliance.

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:23.100
What happens when the same sensors later feed performance evaluations or insurance pricing

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:29.140
without fresh consent or accuracy checks? Simply because the cameras and data streams are already

00:09:29.140 --> 00:09:35.960
installed and inexpensive to repurpose. That's the case for today. The lessons tend to travel further

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:41.520
when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're

